1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,818
This is Linda.

2
00:00:00,818 --> 00:00:05,667
She's fifty-eight, she lives in Des Moines, and she cleans houses ten hours a week.

3
00:00:05,667 --> 00:00:07,361
In October, a letter arrives.

4
00:00:07,361 --> 00:00:14,781
Since the 2025 federal law, she must prove eighty hours a month of work, training or volunteering, or lose her food assistance.

5
00:00:14,781 --> 00:00:16,183
She has twenty-one days.

6
00:00:16,183 --> 00:00:17,293
And she has no car.

7
00:00:17,793 --> 00:00:18,766
Here's the thing.

8
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:21,685
The law already counts volunteering, hour for hour.

9
00:00:21,685 --> 00:00:23,287
What's missing is the proof.

10
00:00:23,287 --> 00:00:24,145
So we built it.

11
00:00:24,145 --> 00:00:25,290
This is Count Me In.

12
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:27,196
Linda just texts a number.

13
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:28,277
No app, no password.

14
00:00:28,277 --> 00:00:36,009
She sends a photo of her letter, and in seconds she gets it back in three plain sentences: what it says, the deadline, and the one thing to do.

15
00:00:36,009 --> 00:00:37,414
A reminder is already set.

16
00:00:37,914 --> 00:00:40,105
Then, six simple questions, one at a time.

17
00:00:40,105 --> 00:00:41,774
She can answer in her own words.

18
00:00:41,774 --> 00:00:44,434
The AI turns them into facts, but it never decides.

19
00:00:44,434 --> 00:00:47,719
A deterministic rules engine does, and it cites the regulation.

20
00:00:47,719 --> 00:00:49,180
For Linda, the rule applies.

21
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:51,370
Her paid work gives her forty-three hours.

22
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:52,830
She needs thirty-seven more.

23
00:00:53,330 --> 00:01:01,133
Count Me In offers three missions, never more, ranked by real access: a twelve-minute walk, a DART bus line, around her cleaning schedule.

24
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:01,868
She taps one.

25
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:02,264
Booked.

26
00:01:02,764 --> 00:01:07,738
On the day, she scans the QR code at the door, or texts a four-digit code from any basic phone.

27
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:10,827
When she leaves, Pastor Dave confirms her hours in one tap.

28
00:01:10,827 --> 00:01:14,230
That validation is signed and chained in a tamper-evident ledger.

29
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:15,277
And her gauge moves.

30
00:01:15,777 --> 00:01:18,475
At the end of the month, she gets a signed attestation.

31
00:01:18,475 --> 00:01:20,535
Nothing goes to the State without her yes.

32
00:01:20,535 --> 00:01:24,263
A caseworker scans the QR code and sees, in one second, that it's authentic.

33
00:01:24,263 --> 00:01:26,617
Change a single digit, and the signature breaks.

34
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:28,510
And for Iowa, a public map.

35
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:34,236
County by county, where eighty hours are reachable without a car, and where they are mathematically impossible.

36
00:01:34,236 --> 00:01:41,354
With conservative assumptions, five hundred people supported means about three hundred thousand dollars of food aid preserved, every year.

37
00:01:41,854 --> 00:01:46,064
It speaks eight languages, from Spanish to Swahili, by text or by voice.

38
00:01:46,064 --> 00:01:49,164
One hour of volunteering serves three people at once.

39
00:01:49,164 --> 00:01:50,392
Linda keeps her food.

40
00:01:50,392 --> 00:01:52,088
The pantry gains a volunteer.

41
00:01:52,088 --> 00:01:53,667
The State gets clean proof.

42
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:54,895
We don't create food.

43
00:01:54,895 --> 00:01:56,591
We stop it from disappearing.

44
00:01:56,591 --> 00:01:57,292
Count Me In.
